## 1. Chapter Overview — The Big Picture

Evaluation is the hardest part of shipping artificial intelligence applications. This chapter is entirely dedicated to understanding **how** we measure whether an artificial intelligence model or application is actually good.

There are four evaluation families covered in this chapter:

| Family | Description |
|---|---|
| **Language Modeling Metrics** | Understand the underlying language model quality (perplexity, cross entropy) |
| **Exact Evaluation** | Functional correctness and similarity to reference outputs |
| **Artificial Intelligence as a Judge** | Using artificial intelligence to evaluate artificial intelligence responses |
| **Comparative Evaluation** | Ranking models head-to-head using match outcomes |

### Why Evaluation Matters — Real-World Failures Without It

Without proper evaluation, artificial intelligence risk can outweigh artificial intelligence benefit. Documented real-world failures include:

- A man committed suicide after being encouraged by a chatbot
- Lawyers submitted false evidence that was hallucinated by artificial intelligence
- Air Canada was ordered to pay damages when its artificial intelligence chatbot gave a passenger false information

### The Investment Gap in Evaluation

Despite exponential growth in large language model evaluation papers (from 2 papers per month to approximately 35 papers per month in the first half of 2023), evaluation tooling still lags significantly behind modelling and orchestration tools:

- Only approximately 50 of the top 1,000 artificial intelligence repositories on GitHub were dedicated to evaluation (as of May 2024)
- Modelling and training had over 120 repositories
- Anthropic has called on policymakers to increase government funding for evaluation research

> **Key insight from DeepMind:** "Developing evaluations has received little systematic attention compared to developing algorithms." Experiment results are almost exclusively used to improve algorithms and are rarely used to improve evaluation.

