## 2. Why Evaluating Foundation Models Is Hard

Traditional machine learning had close-ended tasks (for example, classify an image → compare against a ground truth label). Foundation models are open-ended. There are four key reasons evaluation became much harder:

### The Four Core Challenges

| Challenge | Explanation | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Intelligence Paradox** | Smarter models are harder to evaluate — evaluators need equal or greater expertise to verify outputs | Few people can verify a PhD-level mathematics solution is correct |
| **Open-endedness** | Infinite valid responses exist for any input — no exhaustive ground truth list can be created | "Comment ça va?" has many valid English translations |
| **Black Box Models** | Architecture, training data, and process are often hidden — evaluators can only observe outputs | GPT-4 does not disclose training data details |
| **Scope Creep** | General-purpose models may perform tasks beyond known benchmarks or even human capabilities | Discovering entirely new tasks that artificial intelligence can do unexpectedly |

### The Benchmark Saturation Problem

Benchmarks become useless once models score perfectly. This has happened repeatedly at accelerating speed:

- **GLUE** (General Language Understanding Evaluation) launched in 2018 → saturated in just 1 year → **SuperGLUE** released in 2019
- **NaturalInstructions** (2021) → replaced by **SuperNaturalInstructions** (2022)
- **Massive Multitask Language Understanding** (2020) → largely replaced by **Massive Multitask Language Understanding Pro** (2024)

> **Bottom line:** Benchmarks need to constantly evolve to keep up with model capabilities, but investment in creating new benchmarks lags far behind investment in building new models.

