## 3. Language Modeling Metrics

Foundation models evolved out of language models. Many foundation models still have a language model as their main component. The performance of this language model component tends to be well correlated with downstream application performance.

All four metrics — cross entropy, perplexity, bits-per-character, and bits-per-byte — measure the same underlying thing from different angles. If you know the value of one, you can compute the other three.

---

### 3.1 Entropy

**Definition:** Entropy measures how much information, on average, a single token carries. Higher entropy means more information per token, which also means the language is harder to predict.

**Intuition:** If you can perfectly predict what someone will say next, what they say carries no new information (zero entropy).

**Example:**

| Language | Number of Tokens | Entropy | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2-token language (upper / lower position) | 2 | 1 bit | Easy to predict — only 1 binary choice |
| 4-token language (upper-left / upper-right / lower-left / lower-right) | 4 | 2 bits | Harder to predict — 4 possible choices |

---

### 3.2 Cross Entropy

**Definition:** Cross entropy measures how difficult it is for the language model to predict what comes next in a dataset. It has two components:

1. The **predictability** of the training data, measured by the training data's entropy
2. How much the **distribution learned by the model diverges** from the true distribution of the training data

**Formula:**

```
H(P, Q) = H(P) + D_KL(P || Q)
```

Where:
- `P` = the true distribution of the training data
- `Q` = the distribution learned by the language model
- `H(P)` = entropy of the training data
- `D_KL(P || Q)` = Kullback-Leibler divergence (how far the model's distribution is from the true distribution)

**Key properties:**
- Cross entropy is **not symmetric**: `H(P, Q) ≠ H(Q, P)`
- When the model learns perfectly: divergence = 0, so cross entropy = data entropy
- Training minimises cross entropy

---

### 3.3 Bits-per-Character and Bits-per-Byte

Since different models use different tokenisation methods, the number of bits per token is not comparable across models. These normalised metrics solve that problem:

- **Bits-per-Character (BPC)** = bits per token ÷ average number of characters per token
- **Bits-per-Byte (BPB)** = BPC ÷ bytes per character (accounts for different character encoding schemes like ASCII versus UTF-8)

**Why this matters:** If a model has a BPB of 3.43, it can represent each original byte (8 bits) using only 3.43 bits — meaning it can compress the original text to less than half its original size.

---

### 3.4 Perplexity

**Definition:** Perplexity is the exponential of cross entropy. It measures the amount of **uncertainty** the model has when predicting the next token. Higher uncertainty means more possible options for the next token.

**Formula:**
```
PPL(P, Q) = 2^H(P, Q)          [when using bits as the unit]
PPL(P, Q) = e^H(P, Q)          [when using nats, as in TensorFlow and PyTorch]
```

**Intuition example:** If cross entropy = 2 bits, then perplexity = 4. This means the model behaves as if it has 4 equally likely options when predicting the next token. A perplexity of 3 out of a vocabulary of 100,000 tokens is extraordinary — the model has effectively narrowed the field from 100,000 to just 3 candidates.

---

### 3.5 Rules of Thumb for Interpreting Perplexity

| Factor | Effect on Perplexity | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| More structured data (for example, HTML code) | Lower perplexity | Opening tag predicts closing tag — more predictable |
| Larger vocabulary | Higher perplexity | More possible next tokens to choose from |
| Longer context window | Lower perplexity | More context = less uncertainty about what comes next |
| Post-training (Supervised Fine-Tuning / Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) | Higher perplexity | Post-training "collapses entropy" — model learns to complete tasks, not predict tokens |
| Quantization | Unpredictable change | Reduces numerical precision in unexpected ways |

---

### 3.6 Practical Uses of Perplexity Beyond Training

Beyond guiding model training, perplexity has several practical applications in the artificial intelligence engineering workflow:

1. **Model capability proxy** — Lower perplexity correlates with better downstream task performance (confirmed by OpenAI's GPT-2 paper: larger models consistently showed lower perplexity across all datasets)
2. **Data contamination detection** — If a model has suspiciously low perplexity on a benchmark's data, that benchmark was likely included in training data, making benchmark results less trustworthy
3. **Training data deduplication** — Only add new data to the training dataset if the model's perplexity on that data is high (meaning it has not seen it before)
4. **Abnormal text detection** — Gibberish and unusual sentences produce very high perplexity

> **Reference numbers from OpenAI's GPT-2 paper:**
> - 117 million parameter model: WikiText-2 perplexity = 29.41
> - 1,542 million parameter model: WikiText-2 perplexity = 18.34
> Larger model = lower perplexity = better performance

---

### 3.7 Important Limitation of Perplexity

> **Warning:** Perplexity is a poor proxy for evaluating models that have been post-trained using Supervised Fine-Tuning or Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback. As a model gets better at completing tasks, it often gets worse at predicting the next token, so perplexity typically **increases** after post-training even though real-world performance **improves**. Some researchers describe this as post-training "collapsing entropy." Never use perplexity to evaluate instruction-tuned or chat models.

