## 4. Exact Evaluation

Exact evaluation produces **unambiguous scores** — there is no subjectivity involved. Two main approaches exist: Functional Correctness and Similarity Measurements.

---

### 4.1 Functional Correctness

**Definition:** Evaluating whether the system actually performs its intended functionality. This is the gold standard — the ultimate measure of real-world performance.

**Examples by task type:**

| Task | How Functional Correctness Is Measured |
|---|---|
| Code generation | Does the code compile and produce correct outputs? Measured with unit tests |
| Text-to-SQL generation | Does the generated SQL query return correct results when executed? |
| Game-playing bots | Score achieved in the game (for example, Tetris score) |
| Scheduling and optimisation agents | Measured by energy saved, cost reduced, and so on |

**The pass@k metric for code generation:**
- For each problem, generate `k` code samples
- A problem is "solved" if any of the `k` samples pass all test cases
- Final score = percentage of problems solved
- Because more samples give more chances to solve a problem: `pass@1 < pass@3 < pass@10`

**Key benchmarks using functional correctness:**
- **HumanEval** (OpenAI) — code generation
- **Mostly Basic Python Problems Dataset** (Google) — code generation
- **Spider**, **BIRD-SQL**, **WikiSQL** — text-to-SQL generation

---

### 4.2 Similarity Measurements Against Reference Data

When functional correctness cannot be automated, compare artificial intelligence outputs against human-curated reference responses.

**The four similarity measurement methods:**

| Method | How It Works | Best For | Key Limitation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Exact Match | Generated response must exactly match a reference response | Short factual answers, simple mathematics, trivia | Fails completely for open-ended or multi-answer tasks |
| Lexical Similarity | Measures overlapping tokens or n-grams (BLEU, ROUGE, METEOR) | Translation, summarisation | Misses semantically correct paraphrases; high score ≠ correct answer |
| Semantic Similarity | Cosine similarity of vector embeddings (BERTScore, MoverScore) | Complex language tasks | Quality depends entirely on the underlying embedding model |
| Human or Artificial Intelligence Evaluator | Ask a person or artificial intelligence model to compare responses | Subjective quality judgements | Slow and expensive (human) or biased and inconsistent (artificial intelligence) |

---

### 4.3 Lexical Similarity — Key Metrics in Detail

**How n-gram overlap works:**
- A 1-gram (unigram) is a single token
- A 2-gram (bigram) is a pair of consecutive tokens. For example, "my cats scare the mice" contains the bigrams: "my cats", "cats scare", "scare the", "the mice"
- Lexical similarity measures what percentage of n-grams in the reference response also appear in the generated response

**Common metrics:**

| Metric | Focus | Common Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy) | Precision — how much of the generated output appears in the reference | Machine translation |
| ROUGE (Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation) | Recall — how much of the reference appears in the generated output | Text summarisation |
| METEOR++ | Adds stemming, synonym matching, paraphrasing awareness | Translation with linguistic flexibility |
| Edit Distance / Fuzzy Matching | Counts insertions, deletions, and substitutions needed to convert one text to the other | Spell-checking, string matching |
| CIDEr (Consensus-based Image Description Evaluation) | Image-captioning-specific n-gram metric | Image captioning |

> **Critical finding from OpenAI (HumanEval benchmark):** BLEU scores for **correct** and **incorrect** code solutions were nearly identical. This proves that optimising for BLEU scores is not the same as optimising for functional correctness. Do not use lexical similarity to evaluate code generation tasks.

---

### 4.4 Semantic Similarity — How It Works

Semantic similarity aims to measure whether two texts have the same **meaning**, not just the same words.

**Step 1:** Convert text to an **embedding** — a numerical vector representation that captures meaning. Example: "the cat sits on a mat" → [0.11, 0.02, 0.54, ...]

**Step 2:** Compute **cosine similarity** between two embedding vectors A and B:

```
cosine_similarity(A, B) = (A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)
```

Where:
- `A · B` = dot product of vectors A and B
- `||A||` = Euclidean norm (also called L2 norm) of A
- Range: -1 (completely opposite) to 1 (identical)

**Common embedding models:**

| Model | Embedding Size | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Google BERT (base) | 768 dimensions | Open source, widely used |
| Google BERT (large) | 1,024 dimensions | More powerful version |
| OpenAI text-embedding-3-small | 1,536 dimensions | Strong general-purpose, API-based |
| OpenAI text-embedding-3-large | 3,072 dimensions | Highest quality, more expensive |
| Cohere Embed version 3 (English) | 1,024 dimensions | Strong multilingual alternative |

**Metrics for semantic textual similarity:**
- **BERTScore** — embeddings generated by BERT
- **MoverScore** — embeddings generated by a mixture of algorithms

**Limitation:** Semantic similarity can also be considered subjective because different embedding algorithms produce different embeddings. Two texts with identical meaning can still receive a low similarity score if the embedding model is poor quality.

---

### 4.5 Introduction to Embeddings

An embedding is a **numerical vector representation** that aims to capture the meaning of the original data. Real embedding vectors typically have between 100 and 10,000 dimensions.

**Joint embeddings (multimodal):** Models like CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training) can map data of different modalities — text and images — into a shared embedding space. This enables text-based image search: find images whose embeddings are closest to the embedding of a text query.

**Other joint embedding models:**
- **ULIP** (Unified Language-Image-Points) — unifies text, images, and 3-dimensional point clouds
- **ImageBind** — learns joint embeddings across six different modalities including text, images, and audio

