## 6. Ranking Models with Comparative Evaluation

Instead of scoring models in isolation (pointwise), **pit them against each other** and infer rankings from win/loss records — similar to the Elo rating system in chess.

---

### 6.1 Pointwise versus Comparative Evaluation

| Aspect | Pointwise Evaluation | Comparative Evaluation |
|---|---|---|
| Method | Score each model independently | Compare models head-to-head in matches |
| Analogy | Gymnastics: judge gives each routine a numerical score | Dance contest: judges pick the better dancer side-by-side |
| Strength | Gives absolute scores — answers "is this model good enough?" | Easier for subjective quality; harder to cheat |
| Weakness | Difficult to give absolute scores for open-ended outputs | Does not tell you if any model is actually good enough |
| Requires | Well-designed benchmarks and scoring rubrics | Rating algorithm (Elo, Bradley-Terry, TrueSkill) |

---

### 6.2 How Comparative Evaluation Works

1. Two models are selected per match and generate responses to the same prompt
2. An evaluator — human or artificial intelligence — picks the winner or declares a tie
3. Win rates are computed per model pair across many matches
4. A rating algorithm converts win rates into a ranking

**Important history:**
- Comparative evaluation was first used in artificial intelligence in **2021 by Anthropic** to rank different models
- **LMSYS Chatbot Arena** is the most prominent example — originally used Elo rating, later switched to **Bradley-Terry** algorithm because Elo proved too sensitive to the order of matches and prompts
- January 2024: 57 models evaluated across 244,000 comparisons (approximately 153 comparisons per model pair)

---

### 6.3 Challenges of Comparative Evaluation

**Scalability bottlenecks**
- The number of model pairs grows quadratically with the number of models
- 57 models = 1,596 unique model pairs
- Rating algorithms assume **transitivity** (if A beats B and B beats C, then A should beat C), but human preference is not necessarily transitive

**Lack of standardisation and quality control**
- Crowdsourced comparisons (like LMSYS Chatbot Arena) allow anyone to vote — evaluators may not fact-check responses
- Simple prompts dominate: among 33,000 LMSYS Arena prompts in 2023, 180 were just "hello" or "hi"
- Simple prompts cannot differentiate strong models from weak ones

**From comparative to absolute performance**
- Comparative evaluation tells you A is better than B — it does not tell you whether A is actually good enough for your use case
- A 51 percent win rate of model B over model A does not tell you how much real-world performance will improve

---

### 6.4 Why Comparative Evaluation Still Has a Future

Despite its limitations, comparative evaluation has several irreplaceable advantages:

- It is **easier for evaluators** than giving absolute scores — even when models produce outputs beyond human ability, evaluators can still detect which is better
- It **never saturates** — unlike benchmarks that become useless at perfect scores, comparative evaluation remains meaningful as long as new models exist
- It **captures human preference directly** — the quality metric that ultimately matters for user-facing applications
- It is **hard to cheat** — no fixed reference data exists to train on

> **Important distinction:** Comparative evaluation is NOT the same as A/B testing. In A/B testing, each user sees the output from ONE model at a time. In comparative evaluation, the user sees outputs from MULTIPLE models simultaneously.

