## 7. Quick-Reference Cheat Sheet

### Metric Hierarchy

| Metric | Type | What It Measures | Key Weakness |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cross Entropy | Language model | Difficulty of predicting training data tokens | Does not directly measure downstream task performance |
| Perplexity | Language model | Uncertainty in predicting the next token | Unreliable after Supervised Fine-Tuning or Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback post-training |
| Bits-per-Character / Bits-per-Byte | Language model | Compression efficiency (normalised across tokenisers) | Cross-model comparison still requires same tokenisation approach |
| Exact Match | Exact | Binary match to a reference response | Fails for any paraphrasing or multi-answer tasks |
| BLEU / ROUGE | Lexical | n-gram overlap with a reference response | High score does not equal correct code or genuinely good quality |
| BERTScore | Semantic | Cosine similarity of BERT embeddings | Quality entirely depends on the underlying embedding model |
| Artificial Intelligence Judge Score | Subjective | Holistic quality per the judge's criteria | Biased, inconsistent, not standardised across tools |
| Elo / Bradley-Terry | Comparative | Relative model ranking from match wins | Does not indicate whether any model meets an absolute quality bar |

---

### Artificial Intelligence Judge Biases

| Bias | Description | Who Exhibits It |
|---|---|---|
| Self-bias | Prefers its own outputs — GPT-4 at +10 percent win rate, Claude version 1 at +25 percent win rate for own responses | All models tested |
| First-position bias | Prefers the FIRST response in pairwise comparisons | Artificial intelligence models generally |
| Verbosity bias | Prefers longer answers even if factually wrong compared to shorter correct answers | GPT-3.5 strongly; GPT-4 to a lesser extent |
| Recency bias | Prefers the LAST response seen — the opposite of artificial intelligence position bias | Humans (not artificial intelligence) |

