## 8. Common Exam Traps and Things to Remember

### Trap 1 — Perplexity Does Not Mean Better Task Performance After Post-Training

After Supervised Fine-Tuning or Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback, a model can get **worse** at predicting next tokens (higher perplexity) but **better** at answering questions. Post-training "collapses entropy." Never use perplexity to evaluate instruction-tuned or chat models.

---

### Trap 2 — High BLEU Score Does Not Mean Correct Code

OpenAI showed on HumanEval that BLEU scores for **correct** and **incorrect** code solutions were nearly identical. Optimising for BLEU is not the same as optimising for actually solving problems correctly. Lexical similarity is a weak proxy for functional correctness.

---

### Trap 3 — Scores from Different Artificial Intelligence Judges Are Not Comparable

MLflow "faithfulness" (1 to 5), Ragas "faithfulness" (0 or 1), and LlamaIndex "faithfulness" (Yes or No) all measure different things using different prompts and different criteria. Never compare raw scores across tools without inspecting the underlying judge configuration.

---

### Trap 4 — Comparative Evaluation Is Not the Same as A/B Testing

In A/B testing: each user sees ONE model's output at a time. In comparative evaluation: the evaluator sees outputs from BOTH models simultaneously and picks the better one. These methodologies answer fundamentally different questions.

---

### Trap 5 — Artificial Intelligence Position Bias Is the Opposite of Human Bias

Humans have **recency bias** — they tend to prefer the answer they see **last**. Artificial intelligence models have **first-position bias** — they tend to prefer the answer that appears **first**. In pairwise evaluations, always randomise the order of responses and run each comparison twice to control for this bias.

---

### Trap 6 — Stronger Models Are Not Always the Best Judges

Using a stronger model as a judge is expensive and introduces self-bias at the top of the hierarchy (the strongest model has no eligible judge above it). Small, specialised judges — trained to make specific judgements using specific criteria — can be more reliable than large general-purpose judges for specific tasks.

---

### Trap 7 — Reference Data Quality Matters as Much as the Metric

The WMT 2023 Metrics shared task organisers found **many bad reference translations** in their evaluation data. Low-quality reference data makes reference-based metrics unreliable. This is one reason reference-free metrics (like artificial intelligence judges) have gained popularity — they do not depend on the quality of curated reference data.

